<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<object type="traktor.mcp.SkillAsset">
	<name>edit-skinned-rig</name>
	<description>Add or modify joints, geometry, skin weights and animation-take poses on an existing skinned character in place, without regenerating it.</description>
	<whenToUse><![CDATA[Asked to extend or edit an existing skinned/animated character or creature — add joints (e.g. forearms, hands, feet), re-skin part of the mesh, or add joints to existing animation takes — while preserving the current geometry and animation clips. For building a character from scratch, use create-animated-character instead.]]></whenToUse>
	<body><![CDATA[# Edit an existing skinned rig in place

Extend or modify the skinned character "{{mesh}}" (add joints, re-skin geometry, and/or add joints to its animation takes) **without regenerating it**, so existing geometry and clips are preserved. This is the in-place counterpart to *create-animated-character* (use that to build one from scratch). It relies on the `model_*` tools, which read and write an existing `.tmd` directly.

## Golden rule
Edit the model **in place**: open → mutate → save `overwriteSource`. Do NOT rebuild via `create_mesh_from_geometry`: that path cannot read back existing keyframe poses, so it silently destroys hand-made animation. Only the `model_*` tools can extend a rig non-destructively.

## Pipeline
`model_open {meshAsset}` → `model_get_elements` (read) / `model_edit` (mutate) / `model_apply_operation` → `model_save {overwriteSource}` → `build_asset` the mesh + SkeletonAsset + every AnimationAsset.

## 1. Open and map the existing rig
- `model_open { "meshAsset": "{{mesh}}" }` → `handle`. `model_inspect` gives counts plus the joint and animation lists.
- `model_get_elements` kinds: `joints`, `positions`, `vertices`, `polygons`, and `pose` (needs `animation`+`keyFrame`).
- A box-man is **one rigid box per joint**: 24 vertices (6 faces × 4) + 6 quads, each vertex weighted 1.0 to a single joint. Box *k* usually occupies positions/vertices `24k..24k+23` and polygons `12k..12k+11` (verify).

## 2. Add joints (`model_edit` `addJoint`)
- Joints are **appended** at the end (indices keep growing); existing pose/vertex data indexed 0..n-1 stays valid.
- `translation` is **LOCAL** = `worldChild − worldParent`; `rotation` `[0,0,0,1]`; `parent` = an existing joint index.
- Make new joints **children of the joints already animated** (forearm→upper-arm, foot→shin) so existing takes carry them along.

## 3. Re-skin so new joints deform the mesh (optional)
Rigid box-man workflow, all additive:
- `removePolygons {indices}` for the box(es) you're replacing. Orphaned positions/vertices are harmless (unreferenced = not drawn).
- Append new boxes:
  - `addPosition`: 8 corners.
  - `addNormal`: reuse the 6 face normals ±X/±Y/±Z.
  - `addVertex`: 24, each with `position`, `normal`, `jointInfluences:[{joint,weight:1.0}]`.
  - `addPolygon`: 6 quads, `material:0`, `normal:-1`.
- **Winding**: a face is front-facing when `cross(p2−p0, p1−p0)` points outward (counter-clockwise seen from outside in Traktor's left-handed view). Auto-orient each face: if `dot(cross(p1−p0, p2−p0), outwardNormal) > 0`, reverse the vertex order.
- Polygons store `normal:-1`; lighting uses per-vertex normals, so give each vertex the true **outward** normal.
- `model_apply_operation` `Triangulate` after adding quads.
- Indices are deterministic (appends start at the current counts). Add in batches positions→normals→vertices→polygons and check the returned counts between batches. Generate the arrays with a script to avoid index/winding mistakes.

## 4. Key the new joints in the animation takes
- `model_edit` `setPoseJoint {animation, keyFrame, joint}` with no `translation`/`rotation` writes the joint's **bind** transform. Pass `rotation` (and/or `translation`) to articulate it.
- Any gap below the keyed joint is filled with bind transforms, so order doesn't matter. Other joints keep their exact curves.
- **Key a joint in every keyframe of a take, or in none.** A joint present in no keyframe keeps its bind offset (identity local rotation). A joint keyed in some keyframes but not others collapses onto its parent in the frames where it is missing.
- Read a pose back with `model_get_elements kind=pose`; `total` should reach the skeleton joint count.

## 5. Verify, then commit
- Save to a **throwaway** asset first: `model_save { meshAsset: { path:"Models/X_check", fileName:"Models/X_check.tmd", meshType:"skinned" } }` → `render_mesh_preview` it to check shape and winding. It rasterizes the source model in **bind pose** only; no build needed.
- When good, `model_save { overwriteSource: true }` to write the real `.tmd`. Delete the temp instance (`delete_instance`) and its file.

## 6. Rebuild
`build_asset` (rebuild) the mesh, the `SkeletonAsset`, and **each** `AnimationAsset`. They all import from the `.tmd`, so new joints and pose entries only reach runtime after rebuilding.

## Gotchas (hard-won)
- **Partially keyed joint → collapses onto its parent** in the unkeyed frames (step 4). Render preview won't catch it because it only shows the bind pose.
- **Skeleton↔take matching is by joint NAME** in the pipeline. A rename or typo logs `No such joint` and holds that skeleton joint at its bind pose. Keep names stable between skeleton and takes.
- **No `removeAnimation`/`removeKeyFrame`** in the Model API: you can only add/edit poses and keyframes.
- **Winding/weights** as in step 3, the same rules as *create-animated-character*.
- There is no MCP tool to render an animated frame, so runtime animation must be checked by launching the game from the editor.]]></body>
	<engineVersion/>
	<published>true</published>
	<parameters>
		<item type="traktor.mcp.SkillParameter">
			<name>mesh</name>
			<description>MeshAsset path or guid of the character to edit</description>
			<defaultValue>Models/Humanoid</defaultValue>
		</item>
	</parameters>
</object>
